"Rainbow Induced Subgraphs in Replication Graphs | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/1201.5340)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; @misc{szykuła2012rainbowinducedsubgraphsreplication, title={Rainbow Induced Subgraphs in Replication Graphs}, author={Marek Szykuła and Andrzej Kisielewicz}, year={2012}, eprint={1201.5340}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.DM}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.5340}, }\nSubmitted 25 Jan 2012\n\n# Rainbow Induced Subgraphs in Replication Graphs\n\n## Abstract\n\nA graph $G$G is called a replication graph of a graph $H$H if $G$G is obtained from $H$H by replacing vertices of $H$H by arbitrary cliques of vertices and then replacing each edge in $H$H by all the edges between corresponding cligues. For a given graph $H$H the $\\rho_R(H)$ρ R​(H) is the minimal number of vertices of a replication graph $G$G of $H$H such that every proper vertex coloring of $G$G contains a rainbow induced subgraph isomorphic to $H$H having exactly one vertex in each replication clique of $G$G. We prove some bounds for $\\rho_R$ρ R​ for some classes of graphs and compute some exact values. Also some experimental results obtained by a computer search are presented and conjectures based on them are formulated.\n\nView PaperPDF\n\n## BibTeX\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{szykuła2012rainbowinducedsubgraphsreplication, title={Rainbow Induced Subgraphs in Replication Graphs}, author={Marek Szykuła and Andrzej Kisielewicz}, year={2012}, eprint={1201.5340}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.DM}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.5340}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRainbow Induced Subgraphs in Replication Graphs｜arXiv 1201.5340｜arXivDaily (https://www.arxivdaily.com/papers/1201.5340)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.8 years ago; Crawled: last week; arXiv 1201.5340 2012-01-25 cs.DM math.CO\n\n# Rainbow Induced Subgraphs in Replication Graphs\n\narXiv 1201.5340 2012-01-25 cs.DM math.CO\n\n# Rainbow Induced Subgraphs in Replication Graphs\n\nMarek Szykuła, Andrzej Kisielewicz\n\nA graph $G$ is called a replication graph of a graph $H$ if $G$ is obtained from $H$ by replacing vertices of $H$ by arbitrary cliques of vertices and then replacing each edge in $H$ by all the edges between corresponding cligues. For a given graph $H$ the $ρ_R(H)$ is the minimal number of vertices of a replication graph $G$ of $H$ such that every proper vertex coloring of $G$ contains a rainbow induced subgraph isomorphic to $H$ having exactly one vertex in each replication clique of $G$. We prove some bounds for $ρ_R$ for some classes of graphs and compute some exact values. Also some experimental results obtained by a computer search are presented and conjectures based on them are formulated.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Kisielewicz_A in:cs - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AKisielewicz_A+in%3Acs)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; ~Kisielewicz}, title = {{T}he {C}erny conjecture for automata respecting intervals of a directed graph}, year = {2012}, eprint = {1207.2556}, note = {arXiv:1207.2556v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ...   * Rainbow Induced Subgraphs in Replication Graphs ... @misc{1201.5340, author = {Marek Szykuła and Andrzej Kisielewicz}, title = {{R}ainbow {I}nduced {S}ubgraphs in {R}eplication {G}raphs}, year = {2012}, eprint = {1201.5340}, note = {arXiv:1201.5340v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\n  * Rainbow Induced Subgraphs in Replication Graphs\n\nMarek Szykuła, Andrzej Kisielewicz\n\nJan 26 2012 cs.DM math.CO arXiv:1201.5340v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{1201.5340, author = {Marek Szykuła and Andrzej Kisielewicz}, title = {{R}ainbow {I}nduced {S}ubgraphs in {R}eplication {G}raphs}, year = {2012}, eprint = {1201.5340}, note = {arXiv:1201.5340v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nA graph $G$ is called a replication graph of a graph $H$ if $G$ is obtained from $H$ by replacing vertices of $H$ by arbitrary cliques of vertices and then replacing each edge in $H$ by all the edges between corresponding cligues. For a given graph $H$ the $\\rho_R(H)$ is the minimal number of vertices of a replication graph $G$ of $H$ such that every proper vertex coloring of $G$ contains a rainbow induced subgraph isomorphic to $H$ having exactly one vertex in each replication clique of $G$. We prove some bounds for $\\rho_R$ for some classes of graphs and compute some exact values. Also some experimental results obtained by a computer search are presented and conjectures based on them are formulated.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSet Systems Containing Many Maximal Chains | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/1309.4643)\nciteturn3search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; We conjecture that a `tower of cubes' construction is extremal. ... @misc{johnson2013setsystemscontainingmaximal, title={Set Systems Containing Many Maximal Chains}, author={J. ... Russell}, year={2013}, eprint={1309.4643}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, doi={https://doi.org/10.1017/S0963548314000510}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.4643}, }\nSubmitted 18 Sept 2013\n\n# Set Systems Containing Many Maximal Chains\n\nJR\n\nJ. Robert Johnson\n\nIL\n\nImre Leader\n\nPA\n\nPaul A. Russell\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe purpose of this short problem paper is to raise an extremal question on set systems which seems to be natural and appealing. Our question is: which set systems of a given size maximise the number of $(n+1)$(n+1)-element chains in the power set $\\mathcal{P}(\\{1,2,\\dots,n\\})$P({1,2,…,n})? We will show that for each fixed $\\alpha>0$α>0 there is a family of $\\alpha 2^n$α 2 n sets containing $(\\alpha+o(1))n!$(α+o(1))n! such chains, and that this is asymptotically best possible. For smaller set systems we are unable to answer the question. We conjecture that a `tower of cubes' construction is extremal. We finish by mentioning briefly a connection to an extremal problem on posets and a variant of our question for the grid graph.\n\nView Paper\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{johnson2013setsystemscontainingmaximal, title={Set Systems Containing Many Maximal Chains}, author={J. Robert Johnson and Imre Leader and Paul A. Russell}, year={2013}, eprint={1309.4643}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, doi={https://doi.org/10.1017/S0963548314000510}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.4643}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExtremality of generalized towers of cubes for maximal chains — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/186117/extremality-of-generalized-towers-of-cubes-for-maximal-chain)\nciteturn3search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Extremality of generalized towers of cubes for maximal chains\n\nConjecture open ... Source: https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.4643\n\nExtremality of generalized towers of cubes for maximal chains\n\nConjecture open\n\nLet $X=\\{1,2,\\dots,n\\}$X={1,2,…,n} and let $\\mathcal{P}(X)$P(X) be its power set. A generalized tower of cubes is a family\n\n$$\\mathcal{T}=\\{A:(X_1\\cup\\dots\\cup X_s)\\subseteq A\\subseteq(X_1\\cup\\dots\\cup X_{s+1})\\text{ for some }0\\leq s\\leq k-1\\},$$T={A:(X 1​∪⋯∪X s​)⊆A⊆(X 1​∪⋯∪X s+1​) for some 0≤s≤k−1},\n\nwhere $X_1,\\dots,X_k$X 1​,…,X k​ are pairwise disjoint subsets of $X$X whose union is $X$X, and each $|X_i|$∣X i​∣ equals $t$t or $t+1$t+1. For $\\mathcal{A}\\subseteq\\mathcal{P}(X)$A⊆P(X), let $c(\\mathcal{A})$c(A) denote the number of maximal chains in $\\mathcal{A}$A. Generalized tower-of-cubes conjecture. For values of $|\\mathcal{A}|$∣A∣ which allow such a construction, if $|\\mathcal{A}|=|\\mathcal{T}|$∣A∣=∣T∣, then\n\n$$c(\\mathcal{A})\\leq c(\\mathcal{T}).$$c(A)≤c(T).\n\nThis extends the proposed tower-of-cubes extremality to sizes obtained using cubes of dimensions $t$t and $t+1$t+1; the claim remains conjectural.\n\n## References\n\nSource: https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.4643\n\nStatus: open according to the source (as of September 2013). “In this direction Kleitman proved the following quantitative extension of Sperner's theorem: the minimum number of comparable pairs is achieved by a union of middle layers of $\\ps$\\ps.”\n\nboolean-latticeextremal-combinatoricsset-systems\n\n0\n\nposted by arXiv 3 days ago source: arXiv:1309.4643\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA brief conversation about subtraction games (https://library.slmath.org/books/Book71/files/GONC6-02.pdf)\nciteturn3search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; Combinatorial Theory* **1** (1966), 443–458. ... Post, “Superpolynomial period lengths of the winning positions in the subtraction game”, 2023. arXiv 2312.02426 ... Slusky, “Subtraction games with FES sets of size 3”, 2012. arXiv 1201.3299 ... Ward, “A conjecture about periods in subtraction games”, 2016. arXiv 1606.04029 ... Department of Informatics, Information Science and Electrical Engineering, Kyushu University, Fukuoka, Japan\nA* **58**:1 (1991), 1–25. MR Zbl  \n[14] S. W. Golomb, “A mathematical investigation of games of “take-away””, *J. Combinatorial Theory* **1** (1966), 443–458. MR Zbl  \n[15] N. B. Ho, “On the expansion of three-element subtraction sets”, *Theoret. Comput. Sci.* **582** (2015), 35–47. MR Zbl  \n[16] U. Larsson and J. Wästlund, “From heaps of matches to the limits of computability”, *Electron. J. Combin.* **20**:3 (2013), art. id. 41. MR Zbl  \n[17] U. Larsson, I. Saha, and M. Yokoo, “Subtraction games in more than one dimension”, *Theoret. Comput. Sci.* **1016** (2024), art. id. 114775. MR Zbl  \n[18] I. Miklós and L. Post, “Superpolynomial period lengths of the winning positions in the subtraction game”, 2023. arXiv 2312.02426  \n[19] R. J. Nowakowski, “Unsolved problems in combinatorial games”, pp. 125–168 in *Games of